rt  floor and you know we were we're sleep  on the dirt floor as many people in this  community were getting bit by fleas  every night and and I woke up and that  day you know we didn't even have enough  money for food let alone medicine to  treat Chris at the time was go ahead was  suffering from Giardia which you  actually is a parasite you get from the  drinking water there because you're  actually getting the water out of the  out of a pipe out of the side of the  hill and it just hit us so hard that you  know all of our neighbors who work in  this informal economy let on average  make less than a dollar a day have such  a it's such a difficult thing to manage  that small amount of money to not only  pay for your food but for your medicine  for your kids education for all of these  costs that we deal with every day back  in the states but you know they have to  be incredibly innovative to manage that  amount of money to survive and to  replicate that correct me if I'm wrong  you guys to replicate the kind of  uncertainty in terms of you might a ver  Adal or a day over a month but you might  not be getting a dollar every day it  talked to us about how you guys  simulated that right so the statistic is  really misleading actually so that this  dollar a day statistic you know it's  actually an average over a year so it  comes from the fact that most people  work in the informal economy so they  might you know someday they might have  work one week they might not have work  you know people are working as farmers  they're working as day laborers so what  we did is we took our $56 each we're  there for 56 days and we split that into  random numbers between zero and nine and  then we would draw from a hat each day  that number that would be our income for  the day and you know some days we would  draw very low numbers and there's a week  where we're actually living on less than  12 cents a day and what this replicated  was you know that people living in  extreme poverty of very irregular  incomes and like I said before they have  to be very innovative to manage that  amount of money and that's something  that really surprised us and people in  this community were actually using all  these informal tools one of our friends  was using a savings group so he'd get  together with twelve of his friends each  week they would put in ten dollars and  one person would take home that one  hundred and twenty dollars and that $120  that lump sum of money would allow them  to invest in things like a stove I'm a  different things that they use all these  different technique  to manage that's come out of money to to  actually survive and in seeing the film  what you guys did I think a great job of  showing is when you're at this level of  income an extra dollar a day can  represent an increase of a hundred  percent of your income so I think that  that's hard for people in in the kind of  developed world who are not living in  poverty to even wrap their minds around  which is that second dollar can be  life-altering for so many people when it  represents a hundred percent or maybe  even more of their daily income  absolutely and it should be incredibly  empowering for us because if we can  provide people with the opportunity to  raise their income by one dollar each  day so they're making two dollars a day  like you said that's doubling their  income and a lot of times you know we we  think of we think of things in such huge  steps how do we you know move someone  completely out of poverty tomorrow but  the reality is these incremental changes  these small impacts in someone's life  can have it can create huge change in  their life and um you know I think one  of the best examples of this is this  woman named Rosa who is actually one of  the stars of our film and you know in  the sixth grade she had to drop out of  elementary school on because her father  became sick and she had to help provide  for her family but she had this dream of  becoming 